'''''Eosteus''''' is an [[Extinction|extinct]] [[Monotypic taxon|monotypic genus]] of basal [[Osteichthyes|bony fish]] that lived in what is now China during the [[Telychian]] [[Stage (stratigraphy)|stage]] of the [[Llandovery Epoch|Llandovery]] [[Geologic time scale|epoch]].

== Description ==
''Eosteus chongqingensis'' was [[fusiform]] in overall body appearance. It possessed caudal fulcra and a single dorsal fin, features characteristic of [[Actinopterygii|actinopterygians]]. It also had pectoral, dorsal, and anal fin spines, serial dorsal plates, and an absence of lepidotrichia, features shared with [[stem group]] [[Chondrichthyes|chondrichthyans]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Zhu |first1=You-An |last2=Chen |first2=Yang |last3=Li |first3=Qiang |last4=Zhao |first4=Wen-Jin |last5=Zhou |first5=Zheng-Da |last6=Jia |first6=Lian-Tao |last7=Yu |first7=Yi-Lun |last8=Yu |first8=Han-Xin |last9=Wei |first9=Guang-Biao |last10=Ahlberg |first10=Per E. |last11=Lu |first11=Jing |last12=Zhu |first12=Min |date=5 March 2026 |title=The oldest articulated bony fish from the early Silurian period |url=https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10125-2#citeas |journal=[[Nature (journal)|Nature]] |language=en |volume=651 |issue=8104 |pages=128–134 |doi=10.1038/s41586-026-10125-2 |issn=0028-0836 |access-date=11 April 2026 |via=Nature Publishing Group|doi-access=free }}</ref>

